# Binaries for programs and plugins
*.exe
*.exe~
*.dll
*.so
*.dylib
*_rpi64
*_imac
*_amac
*_ilin
# Binaries are built with one of the suffixes above — `go build -o name_amac` —
# and not with a bare `go build`, which names the executable after the module
# and leaves something no pattern here matches. A rule to catch that was tried
# and withdrawn: anything general enough to match an unsuffixed file one level
# down also matches modboss/plc, and an ignored directory takes everything
# inside it with it. The suffix is the rule.
*.sh
*.orig

# Test binary, built with `go test -c`
*.test

# Output of the go coverage tool, specifically when used with LiteIDE
*.out

# Dependency directories (remove the comment below to include it)
vendor/
.DS_Store
.vscode/*

# mbaigo
#
# Deployment configuration is a deployment's business: systemconfig.json,
# policies.json, valuation.json and the rest name addresses, rooms and
# judgments that belong to whoever runs the cloud, not to this repository.
*.json
# Except the worked examples, which are documentation. Without this line they
# were silently dropped by `git add`, and a file nobody can see is a poor place
# to keep the copy the tests check against.
!*.example.json
go.work
serviceRegistry.db
*.pem
**/files/
*.pdf
*.xlsx

# A `go build` run inside a system directory writes a binary named after that
# directory: no suffix, so none of the patterns above catch it. Thirty-eight of
# them once reached a commit that way — 393 MB — because the build had been run
# for verification and `git add -A` does not ask what a file is.
#
# Listed from the Makefile's SYSTEMS, so the two cannot drift apart.
/authorizer/authorizer
/beehive/beehive
/beekeeper/beekeeper
/busdriver/busdriver
/ca/ca
/clerk/clerk
/collector/collector
/democrat/democrat
/drafter/drafter
